.\"
.\" Man page for kcore_filtered kernel module
.\"
.TH KCORE_FILTERED 4 "2025-02-08" "Linux" "Linux Kernel Modules"
.SH NAME
kcore_filtered \- privacy-filtered /proc/kcore alternative for safe kernel introspection
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B insmod kcore_filtered.ko
.RI [ parameter = value ]...
.PP
.B modprobe kcore_filtered
.RI [ parameter = value ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kcore_filtered
is a loadable kernel module that creates
.IR /proc/kcore_filtered ,
an ELF core file interface structurally identical to
.IR /proc/kcore
but with page-level privacy filtering.
User process pages (anonymous memory, page cache, free pages) are
redacted to zeroes while kernel data structures remain readable.
.PP
The module is designed for use with
.BR drgn (1)
and similar kernel introspection tools on production systems where
reading arbitrary user memory via
.I /proc/kcore
is not acceptable from a privacy or compliance standpoint.
.PP
On a typical system, filtering removes 80\-95% of sensitive data
while keeping all kernel structures accessible to debugging tools.
.SS Files Created
.TP
.I /proc/kcore_filtered
Main interface.
A read-only ELF64 core file containing filtered kernel memory.
Permissions are 0400 (root read-only).
Opening requires
.BR CAP_SYS_RAWIO .
.TP
.I /proc/kcore_filtered_stats
Read-only statistics file showing per-category page filter counters
and rate-limit event counts.
Permissions are 0400.
.SH PARAMETERS
Parameters can be set at module load time and, unless noted otherwise,
adjusted at runtime via
.IR /sys/module/kcore_filtered/parameters/ .
.SS Layer 0: Page-Level Filtering
These boolean parameters control which page categories are redacted.
All are runtime-writable (0644).
.TP
.BR filter_anon =\fIbool\fR (default: Y)
Filter anonymous user pages (heap, stack, mmap).
These are the primary source of user-private data.
.TP
.BR filter_cache =\fIbool\fR (default: Y)
Filter user file-backed page cache.
These pages contain recently-accessed file contents.
.TP
.BR filter_free =\fIbool\fR (default: Y)
Filter free buddy pages.
Free pages may contain residual data from prior use.
.TP
.BR filter_slab =\fIbool\fR (default: N)
Filter all slab pages.
Slab pages contain kernel objects (task_struct, dentry, inode, etc.)
and are typically needed by drgn.
Setting this to
.B Y
acts as a catch-all kill switch that denies all slab data regardless
of any slab cache list configuration.
.SS Layer 1: Slab Cache Allow/Deny List
Fine-grained per-cache filtering.
Both parameters are load-time only (0444) to avoid re-parsing races.
.TP
.BR slab_action =\fImode\fR (default: allow)
Controls interpretation of
.BR slab_cache_list .
Must be
.B allow
or
.BR deny .
.RS
.TP
.B allow
Only slab caches named in
.B slab_cache_list
pass through; all others are denied.
.TP
.B deny
Only slab caches named in
.B slab_cache_list
are denied; all others pass through.
.RE
.TP
.BR slab_cache_list =\fIname\fR[,\fIname\fR]... (default: empty)
Comma-separated list of slab cache names.
Maximum 64 entries.
When empty, no per-cache filtering is applied.
.PP
The priority hierarchy is:
.IP 1. 4
.B filter_slab=1
overrides everything (deny all slab).
.IP 2.
Non-empty
.B slab_cache_list
applies per-cache filtering using
.BR slab_action .
.IP 3.
Neither set: all slab pages are allowed (default).
.SS Layer 2: Audit Logging
.TP
.BR audit =\fIbool\fR (default: Y)
Emit kernel audit records (type
.BR AUDIT_KERNEL ,
2000) on open, close, denied access, and rate-limit events.
Runtime-writable (0644).
.PP
Records include full task identity via
.BR audit_log_task_info ():
pid, ppid, auid, uid, gid, euid, suid, fsuid, egid, sgid, fsgid,
tty, ses, comm, exe, and LSM security context.
.PP
Close records additionally include
.B bytes_read
and
.BR duration_ms .
.SS Layer 3: Rate Limiting
Per-session and global rate limits to prevent bulk data extraction.
All are runtime-writable (0644).
.TP
.BR max_session_bytes =\fIn\fR (default: 67108864)
Maximum bytes readable per open file descriptor (session).
Once exhausted, subsequent reads return EOF.
Set to 0 to disable.
Default is 64\ MB.
.TP
.BR max_session_secs =\fIn\fR (default: 300)
Maximum session duration in seconds.
After expiry, reads return EOF.
Set to 0 to disable.
.TP
.BR max_opens_per_min =\fIn\fR (default: 10)
Maximum successful opens of
.I /proc/kcore_filtered
per 60-second window across all processes.
Excess opens return
.BR EBUSY .
Set to 0 to disable.
.TP
.BR max_global_bytes_per_min =\fIn\fR (default: 134217728)
Maximum aggregate bytes read per 60-second window across all sessions.
When exceeded, active sessions receive
.B EBUSY
on their next read.
Set to 0 to disable.
Default is 128\ MB.
.SH STATISTICS
The file
.I /proc/kcore_filtered_stats
exposes the following counters:
.TP
.B allowed
Pages served with real kernel data.
.TP
.B denied_anon
Anonymous user pages redacted to zeroes.
.TP
.B denied_cache
File-backed page cache pages redacted.
.TP
.B denied_free
Free buddy pages redacted.
.TP
.B denied_slab
Slab pages redacted (by filter_slab=1 or slab cache list).
.TP
.B denied_offline
Offline or hardware-poisoned pages redacted.
.TP
.B denied_swapbacked
Swap-backed pages redacted.
.TP
.B skipped
Pages not valid or not online (returned as zeroes).
.TP
.B rl_denied_opens
Opens blocked by the global open rate limit.
.TP
.B rl_denied_reads
Reads blocked by the global byte rate limit.
.TP
.B rl_sess_expired
Sessions that hit the time limit.
.TP
.B rl_sess_budget
Sessions that hit the byte budget.
.SH EXAMPLES
Load with default settings (filters anon, cache, and free pages;
allows slab):
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo insmod kcore_filtered.ko
.fi
.RE
.PP
Load with all slab pages filtered:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo insmod kcore_filtered.ko filter_slab=1
.fi
.RE
.PP
Load with a slab allowlist (only listed caches pass through):
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo insmod kcore_filtered.ko slab_action=allow \\
    slab_cache_list=task_struct,dentry,inode_cache,vm_area_struct,mm_struct
.fi
.RE
.PP
Load with a slab denylist (only listed caches are blocked):
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo insmod kcore_filtered.ko slab_action=deny \\
    slab_cache_list=sk_buff_head,skbuff_fclone_cache
.fi
.RE
.PP
Load with custom rate limits:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo insmod kcore_filtered.ko max_session_bytes=33554432 \\
    max_session_secs=120 max_opens_per_min=5
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use with drgn:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo drgn -c /proc/kcore_filtered
.fi
.RE
.PP
Toggle filter_slab at runtime:
.PP
.RS
.nf
echo Y > /sys/module/kcore_filtered/parameters/filter_slab
echo N > /sys/module/kcore_filtered/parameters/filter_slab
.fi
.RE
.PP
View statistics:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo cat /proc/kcore_filtered_stats
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search audit log for access records:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ausearch -m UNKNOWN[2000] -i | grep kcore_filtered
dmesg | grep "kcore_filtered op="
.fi
.RE
.SH PERMISSIONS
.I /proc/kcore_filtered
requires
.B CAP_SYS_RAWIO
to open, the same capability required by
.IR /proc/kcore .
Access attempts without this capability are denied with
.B EPERM
and generate an audit record (if auditing is enabled).
.PP
The module respects kernel lockdown mode.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.B kcore_filtered
targets Linux 6.8 and newer.
It uses only exported GPL symbols and builds as an out-of-tree module
against the running kernel headers.
.PP
Supported architectures: x86-64, ARM64 (with KIMAGE_VADDR support),
and others via fallbacks.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.TP
.B Sub-page problem
Page-level filtering cannot detect user data stored within kernel slab
objects (e.g., data from
.BR copy_from_user ()
stored in kmalloc allocations, network packet payloads in sk_buff
caches).
Use the slab cache allow/deny list to mitigate this for known
high-risk caches.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR drgn (1),
.BR proc (5),
.BR insmod (8),
.BR modprobe (8),
.BR rmmod (8),
.BR ausearch (8)
